    <h2>How Twennie Protects Your Work, Knowledge, and Data</h2>
<p>
Many technical professionals, especially those working in consulting, engineering, science, and project delivery, are rightfully cautious about protecting their intellectual property, work products, and internal knowledge. Twennie is built with this reality in mind. Twennie is a <strong>learning platform</strong> — not a document repository, not a competitor, and not an information-harvesting system. We do not want, request, or accept your proprietary work materials.
</p>

<h3>1. You Own Your Content</h3>
<p>
Anything you write, record, or upload to Twennie remains <strong>your property</strong>. Twennie claims no ownership or rights over the materials you create. You decide whether to share your work privately with your team or publicly with all members. You may delete your content at any time.
</p>

<h3>2. Twennie Never Requests or Requires Proprietary or Confidential Material</h3>
<p>
Twennie does <strong>not</strong> request: project reports, design files, calculations, drawings, client data, bids or proposals, internal processes, financials, or any competitive information. Learning units are skill-based exercises — not submissions of company-sensitive documents. If an assignment or exercise <em>feels</em> like it touches confidential material, we expect and encourage you to generalize, anonymize, or reframe the content. 
</p>

<h3>3. Your Private Notes Are Not Visible to Twennie</h3>
<p>
Notes you record while completing learning units — including reflections, answers to prompts, or documentation of your own progress — are <strong>visible only to you</strong>, and when applicable, to your team’s designated leader. Twennie administrators do <em>not</em> access your private notes or internal observations.
</p>

<h3>4. Twennie Does Not Use Your Content for Any Purpose Without Consent</h3>
<p>
Content you mark as “all-member” may be viewed by other Twennie members for learning purposes only. It will <strong>never</strong> be used in Twennie promotional materials without your explicit consent. Content marked “team-only” remains visible exclusively within your private group unless you manually change those permissions.
</p>

<h3>5. No Competitive Data Is Ever Required</h3>
<p>
Twennie learning activities may ask you to think about client experience, communication, project management, or business development habits — but they never require you to upload client names, pricing, strategies, proprietary workflows, or competitive materials. Members are reminded regularly not to share sensitive or regulated information.
</p>

<h3>6. You Control What You Share</h3>
<p>
Every unit in Twennie can be created in three modes: <strong>private to you</strong>, <strong>shared only with your team</strong>, or <strong>shared with all members</strong>. You choose the audience for every piece of content you create. 
</p>

<h3>7. Twennie Is Not a Repository for Company IP</h3>
<p>
Twennie is not designed to store or manage company intellectual property and should not be used that way. Companies retain full responsibility for their proprietary information. Twennie encourages members to avoid uploading materials that could be sensitive, competitive, or confidential.
</p>

<h3>8. You May Use Twennie Without Sharing Anything at All</h3>
<p>
Many members use Twennie solely for videos, prompt sets, exercises, or templates without submitting a single unit. A full learning experience is possible without creating or sharing any content.
</p>

<h3>9. Private Mode Is Always the Safest Option</h3>
<p>
If you or your organization have heightened risk concerns, we recommend using <strong>private team-only mode</strong> for all uploads and generalizing any project details. This mode ensures your work is accessible only to your immediate learning group.
</p>

<h3>10. Twennie Actively Discourages Sharing Sensitive Information</h3>
<p>
Twennie’s review process is designed to prevent inappropriate submissions. Administrators may decline any uploaded item that includes procurement-sensitive, competitive, confidential, or regulated content. This protects both our members and their employers.
</p>

<h3>Your Trust Matters</h3>
<p>
Twennie exists to help technical professionals learn, collaborate, and grow — <em>not</em> to harvest confidential information. Safeguarding your intellectual property is part of our commitment to maintaining a safe, ethical, and professional learning environment. If you ever have concerns about a planned upload, contact us and we will guide you on how to share your work safely.
</p>

